WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.370
- So I'm gonna talk about inference.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.160
From more or less a quantum
field theory point of view,

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:14.490
I'm gonna attempt to, to
make it very accessible,

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.440
and I think one of the
real problems for physics

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.140
is that quantum field theory
is not accessible enough.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:25.490
So I've spent the last 20 years
focused on renormalization,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.257
but I'm not gonna talk about that at all.

00:00:28.257 --> 00:00:32.763
There are three recent
articles that talk about,

00:00:34.418 --> 00:00:35.880
I'm gonna have to speed up.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.010
That talk about the relationship

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.320
between quantum mechanics
and classical mechanics

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.970
and about the measurement problem.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:43.860
But they do so in terms of,

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.770
and underlying ideas about
quantum field theory,

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.360
and specifically as if it's

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.973
a noisy signal analysis formalism.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.240
And I think that can make
contact with this workshop

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.760
in the sense that inference
and machine learning

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.793
can be thought of as
data analysis toolboxes.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.240
So one can approach the relationship

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.692
between quantum mechanics
and classical mechanics

00:01:05.692 --> 00:01:07.470
in so many different ways.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.990
The way I've chosen to
do for the last few years

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.960
is partly through the
idea that no-go theorems

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.180
tell us that quantum
mechanics is more than,

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.360
in some sense, classical mechanics.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.200
And so one approach following on from that

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.260
is to think that we
should try to construct

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.930
a more complete version
of classical mechanics.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:31.140
Add to it instead of trying
to add to quantum mechanics

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:34.020
so that it's more like
classical mechanics.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.100
And in the articles above,
I construct what I call CM+,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.020
and that includes in the first instance,

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.490
measurement incompatibility.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:44.253
So in algebraic terms, non-commutativity.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.450
You can use the Poisson bracket.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.120
If you are willing to
use it in its full power,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.410
you can use the Poisson bracket

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.770
to construct a full algebra
or non-commutative algebra

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.650
of measurement operators,
measurement observables,

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.406
within classical mechanics.

00:02:00.406 --> 00:02:03.300
It's sort of, it's been strawmanned,

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.066
classical mechanics being strawmanned,

00:02:05.066 --> 00:02:07.803
so that you cannot use
these things just by fiat.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.640
If you do do this, then the
next thing you need to do

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.740
is to introduce the idea, well,

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.720
is to recognize that quantum
noise and thermal noises

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.870
cannot be the same thing
within this new structure.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.380
You need to find some
really solid difference,

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:25.260
and quantum field theory
actually has a solid difference.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:29.160
Quantum noise within quantum field theory

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.220
can be thought of as just the
Lorentz invariant component,

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.210
the vacuum is Lorentz
invariant, and so it's noise,

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:37.110
insofar as you're willing to

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.440
accept that there's noise
there, is Lorentz invariant

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.510
And that, for me, as a field theorist,

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.820
implies that we should really think of CM+

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.200
as a field theory.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.480
So a random field theory
would be a terminology

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:49.413
that can be used.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:51.900
But I'm not going to include into this

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.417
analyticity, what I'm
gonna call analyticity,

00:02:54.417 --> 00:02:57.750
which is a consequence of the
fact that quantum mechanics

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:02.750
rejects the positive
frequencies, which CM+ does not.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.500
And I wanna keep them separate

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:05.880
because I want to be able to compare

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:08.820
CM+ with quantum mechanics,
quantum field theory,

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.770
and see what the differences are

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.953
and hence perhaps learn
something about both.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.280
And indeed this projection
of positive frequency's

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.860
very similar to what you'll
find in signal analysis

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.710
where there is the idea
of an analytic signal

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:26.820
and a real signal essentially
as the positive frequency

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.750
and the not positive and
an unconstrained frequency.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:32.863
Okay, so CM+ is a Hilbert space formalism

00:03:32.863 --> 00:03:36.540
for noisy signal analysis.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.820
And insofar as it is a
Hilbert space formalism,

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:40.230
it can describe experiments

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.080
equally well as quantum field theory.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:46.110
And indeed, for the electromagnetic
field in particular,

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:49.080
not for all such things, we
can construct isomorphisms,

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:52.041
linear isomorphisms, between
quantum electromagnetism

00:03:52.041 --> 00:03:55.023
and what I'm call CM+ electromagnetism.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:59.310
So this is not quantization
because it is isomorphisms.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:00.872
It's a much cleaner construction.

00:04:00.872 --> 00:04:04.080
It's not a replacement for quantization,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.970
but it is an alternative that I suggest

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.833
is useful to have in the toolbox.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:12.510
It's not a panacea, it's
statistically non-local.

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:14.130
This is something that is

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:16.050
going to have consequences down the road

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:19.530
as we try to study what it might do.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:24.420
Okay, so I submitted this
abstract to Priya five weeks ago

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.583
and I've struggled to keep to it.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.220
Correlation's not equal to causality,

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:30.570
obviously, very naive sort of thing.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.010
What do we have to add to it?

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:34.800
Causal modeling does add something to it.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.676
Intervention in particular gives us

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:39.523
a means of talking about causality.

00:04:39.523 --> 00:04:41.730
In mathematics and physics, however,

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:43.950
we have generalized
probability and contextuality,

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:47.100
which gives us similar
tools in a framework

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.460
that is essentially algebraic as a whole,

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.640
and in that sense, I feel
that it's more unified.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:57.120
Obviously this is to people's
taste, to some extent.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:57.960
And for machine learning,

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.180
we'll discover in the last four slides

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:02.730
whether or not I have
something interesting to say.

00:05:03.678 --> 00:05:08.340
So again, there's so many
things you can pick out

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.849
from causal modeling.

00:05:09.849 --> 00:05:12.600
The do-notation, so many
people pick out that,

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.220
well of course I should as well.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.457
In any case, it seems connected to

00:05:16.457 --> 00:05:20.790
the state preparation that
one has in quantum mechanics.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:24.420
So we have required for a transformation

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:27.656
is more or less you take
a dial, you turn it to X,

00:05:27.656 --> 00:05:30.291
there is your, I have willed it so.

00:05:30.291 --> 00:05:32.640
You could have turned it to X1, X2, X3.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:34.470
You didn't, you turned it to X.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:37.680
So do X equals x is semantically different

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.710
in that sense from, it
happened that X equals x.

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.650
There was no will involved at all.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:45.690
But mathematically, it's just the same.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.330
Both of these constructions
can fit perfectly well into

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.583
as models of the Kolmogorov's Axioms.

00:05:52.118 --> 00:05:55.290
So we have experimental protocols

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.450
decree what will be done
by the experimenter.

00:05:57.450 --> 00:05:59.100
The experiment typically is not free.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.560
There is a design of
the experiment that says

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:02.970
you will choose random numbers.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.140
If you choose all zeros,

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.840
then you will have to
do the experiment again.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.030
We can transform any aspect
of an experimental apparatus

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:11.670
by having enough dials, each
one of which is connected

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:15.129
to the internals of the
apparatus so that things happen.

00:06:15.129 --> 00:06:16.470
And afterwards, however,

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.540
there's just data in computer memory.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:20.280
That is what you are going to use.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.235
You're gonna apply any
algorithm that works.

00:06:23.235 --> 00:06:25.800
And you've done multiple interventions

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.660
and you keep track of what
interventions you've done

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:29.940
to obtain that data.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:31.740
So from a data analysis perspective,

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:34.980
one thing we obviously do is
construct relative frequencies.

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:38.460
And for that, we just construct a number

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:40.950
between zero one, but a rational number,

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.800
and we can add those, as long
as we don't double count,

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.840
to get a new relative frequency.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.580
We can add many of them,

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:49.770
so long as we don't double
count or omit anything,

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:51.990
we'll get the real number one.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:55.170
And well, we have to keep
track of double counting

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:56.920
and omission in one way or another.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.060
So historically, the relative frequencies

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:01.650
came out of the 19th century.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:04.320
The Kolmogorov's Axioms
followed that mathematics.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.030
And if it hadn't, we'd be talking about

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:07.683
someone else's axioms, I suggest.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.890
And then once we had
the Kolmogorov's Axioms,

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:12.810
then we construct models for those

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.030
in terms of expected values
for the relative frequencies,

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:16.230
which are now real numbers

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:18.060
instead of rational numbers, perhaps.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:19.410
We can talk about different semantics.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:21.120
So something like propensities.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:22.770
We can talk about the do semantics.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:24.620
And we can also construct
linear algebraic models

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:28.290
for the Kolmogorov's Axioms.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:32.253
So when we construct a, nope, wrong thing,

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:35.430
I've just gone forward a
line that I didn't intend to,

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:36.847
but I won't go back.

00:07:36.847 --> 00:07:39.223
So when we construct for frequencies,

00:07:39.223 --> 00:07:41.940
then we hope that those
will come turn out to be

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.700
the same as what we had from a theory

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:46.080
that we had written down.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.720
Algebraically, we could use compatible,

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:51.840
so commutative operators,
to construct this.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:53.640
Also gives us a positive number,

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:55.830
and so by choosing the right state

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:57.180
and the right projection operators,

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:00.120
we can make this be the same as this.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:04.320
But also we can, if we
want to, use arbitrary,

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.720
so long as the spectrum of the operator,

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.930
no, arbitrary operators at Y and X,

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:12.930
that give us the same numbers as this,

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:14.970
but using non-commutative operators.

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:16.890
And this, in fact, is the mathematics

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:18.990
of the collapse of the wave function,

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:22.440
but just used to construct
a conditional probability,

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:24.940
or indeed of the top line,
just joint probability.

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:29.400
So we have generalized probability

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.920
gives us ways to interpolate
between different contexts.

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:34.782
So if we do find many
more through frequencies

00:08:34.782 --> 00:08:37.727
and we find in many different contexts,

00:08:37.727 --> 00:08:42.727
we place a new context
relative to an earlier context,

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:45.120
then we can compute expected values

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.030
for the new relative frequencies.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.407
And that's what we get out of

00:08:49.407 --> 00:08:52.170
the linear algebra of quantum
mechanics in particular.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:56.220
Incompatibility is a
way of placing context

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:59.403
as non-trivial transformations
of other contexts.

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:03.120
For quantum mechanics, we
can do that straightforwardly

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.060
because we can generate
transformations instantly

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:08.310
just by using any omission operator.

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:09.960
For CM+ it's a little more difficult,

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.649
because we only have commutative algebra,

00:09:12.649 --> 00:09:15.030
but we can use the Poisson bracket

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:16.830
to construct the transformation algebra,

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:20.280
direct transformation
algebra, and then we can use,

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:23.700
so long as we don't strawman ourselves

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:25.590
to construct just the same algebra

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:26.700
as we have from quantum mechanics,

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:28.350
or quantum field theory, in fact.

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:32.190
So I'm gonna talk about
the Bell inequalities,

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:35.220
with apologies, but entirely
from a field theoretic

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:36.570
point of view.

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:38.940
This is an experiment from the 1995.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:42.064
This is page 60 from
Gregor Weihs's thesis.

00:09:42.064 --> 00:09:44.880
It's a really nice diagram
from my point of view,

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:47.457
partly because up here, no, can't,

00:09:49.451 --> 00:09:50.580
I do have to go back from now on.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:53.100
Up here, he talks about a pump.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:56.910
He pumps the electromagnetic
field, as I will say.

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:59.190
He doesn't shovel particles in,

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:00.940
he pumps the electromagnetic field.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:08.000
Then he also is extremely clear
about what he's gonna store.

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:11.326
He's gonna store the time
at which events happen.

00:10:11.326 --> 00:10:15.094
No particles in this at
all, in this description.

00:10:15.094 --> 00:10:16.530
It's not that you can't
have a particle description,

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:17.730
but if you do, then you're gonna have

00:10:17.730 --> 00:10:20.250
quite a compromised idea of
a particle in order to do so.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:21.893
But for a field theory point of view,

00:10:21.893 --> 00:10:24.240
our idea of pumping a noisy field,

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:27.213
it's actually perfectly
straightforward for me.

00:10:28.298 --> 00:10:33.298
So he also, again, with the wrong key.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:39.300
So he also stores which of
these avalanche photodiodes

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:43.800
actually had the signal
went from some low end.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:45.210
I have a beautifully shaky hand,

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:48.450
which is exactly the shaky
as the display there.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:51.810
And when an event happens,
then that's the time

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:53.087
which Gregor stores.

00:10:53.087 --> 00:10:54.880
And at the same time, he also stores

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:57.540
the random bit generated value

00:10:57.540 --> 00:11:00.840
that has set the
electro-optic modulator here,

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:02.760
which is changing the polarization

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:04.835
on a sort of megahertz scale

00:11:04.835 --> 00:11:09.033
so that we can say
something about causality.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:11.910
It needs to be that fast in order for this

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:15.900
to be a good test of that.

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:18.510
On the other hand, this
experiment is much simpler

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:20.460
than the 2016 experiments.

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:24.090
And so I don't want to
use that for display.

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:29.090
So here's the actual data,
my computation of this.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:33.544
This is 15,000 event, oh again.

00:11:33.544 --> 00:11:34.560
15,000 events.

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:36.360
And you can see the coincidence there.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:38.120
Within about one nanosecond,

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:41.670
all of these 15,000 events total across,

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:44.115
all of these plus or
minus three nanoseconds.

00:11:44.115 --> 00:11:47.851
And indeed you can see
that many of the events

00:11:47.851 --> 00:11:50.820
are green and yellow in the
color scheme I've used here,

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:52.650
where the red is the diagonal,

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:54.030
the blue is the lower diagonal,

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:57.360
and the yellow and the green
are the other two diagonals.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:02.340
And that green and yellow
is actually signifies

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:04.530
the violation of inequalities.

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:06.600
If you turn that diagram on its side,

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:09.600
you slice it a hundred times in this way,

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.850
you can compute a histogram that counts

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:13.983
each of the 16 different types of events.

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:18.570
You can add up those numbers,
and then you can put that into

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:21.000
by now completely standard calculation

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.741
of a violation of the Bell inequalities.

00:12:23.741 --> 00:12:26.417
So all we've done here is we have

00:12:26.417 --> 00:12:28.980
a completely classical set of data.

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:31.320
We've applied a completely
classical algorithm,

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:34.050
and out of that we've got
a number at which point,

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:38.215
classical particle physics
says, no, this is not any good.

00:12:38.215 --> 00:12:40.343
We cannot have this.

00:12:40.343 --> 00:12:44.550
So what I'm gonna point to you

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:46.350
is that in quantum mechanics,

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:47.747
we actually use measurement
incompatibility,

00:12:47.747 --> 00:12:51.169
the non-commutativity of
the algebra of measurements

00:12:51.169 --> 00:12:55.031
to model the violation
of Bell inequalities.

00:12:55.031 --> 00:12:56.190
And this is something
you find in literature

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:57.423
since about 1980.

00:12:58.781 --> 00:13:01.200
In classic mechanics as usual,

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:03.600
we don't have measurement incompatibility.

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:06.956
We've strawmanned ourselves,
so we're not allowed to do it.

00:13:06.956 --> 00:13:09.150
One response to this is, well,

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:11.150
you can read what comes next as well,

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:12.840
is we can say that classical mechanics

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:14.343
is computationally incomplete.

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:19.890
Quantum mechanics is more
that classical mechanics.

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:23.010
So we can use the Poisson bracket

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:24.240
to add measurement incompatibility

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:25.760
into classical mechanics, giving us CM+,

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:29.220
so it's computationally as
complete as quantum field theory.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:31.020
It's just a more effective thing

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:33.240
than classical mechanics itself.

00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:36.150
So I've focused on non-commutativity.

00:13:36.150 --> 00:13:37.731
There's clearly a whole pile of literature

00:13:37.731 --> 00:13:39.360
about nonlocality.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:42.600
For quantum fields,
microcausality asserts that

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:44.250
locality is closely associated

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:46.052
with measurement incompatibility.

00:13:46.052 --> 00:13:47.970
And indeed, there's a version of that

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:49.830
which is called holo duality,

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:51.904
which really makes it identical.

00:13:51.904 --> 00:13:54.870
Quantum fields are, however,

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:56.760
are much less like
classical particle physics

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:58.320
than they are like the heat equation

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:01.923
or any kind of non-equilibrium
thermodynamics.

00:14:03.148 --> 00:14:06.180
So those kinds of equate things

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:08.640
do depend on the boundary conditions.

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:10.920
Once you've set the boundary conditions,

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:13.281
the whole system will equilibriate

00:14:13.281 --> 00:14:14.114
to the boundary conditions.

00:14:14.114 --> 00:14:15.900
So completely different
from what one expects

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:18.993
for a particle physics intuition.

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:25.020
And indeed, temperature
measurements in classical,

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:27.570
what effectively is CM+
as far as I'm concerned,

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:29.820
are just sized for measurements
in quantum field theory,

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:33.720
approximately local, but
they are not point-like.

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:35.850
This is something that is standard

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:39.423
in the more mathematical
literature on quantum field theory.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:41.910
So if you're willing to go with me

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:43.230
on the idea that quantum field theory

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:45.210
can be thought of usefully

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:47.880
as a noisy signal analysis formalism,

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:50.400
then I suggest that we do
introduce measurements.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:51.602
This is a measurement theory

00:14:51.602 --> 00:14:54.480
because quantum mechanics
is a measurement theory,

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:56.550
and indeed, quantum field theory is about

00:14:56.550 --> 00:14:58.860
a measurement operator
of value distribution,

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:02.100
which we use to construct
a measurement operator,

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:04.140
which I'm gonna index with something

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:06.000
which I'm gonna label f,

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:07.710
and I'm going to click
through to the next slide

00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:09.065
too soon again.

00:15:09.065 --> 00:15:13.140
Where those two, in
fact, I'm gonna go back

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:14.740
so that I don't distract myself.

00:15:16.145 --> 00:15:18.960
Where the functions f and g,

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:19.980
they're called test functions

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:21.330
in the mathematical literature,

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:24.240
they're called window functions,
much more graphically,

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:26.700
in the signal analysis literature.

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:30.720
These also can be thought
of as the readme functions.

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:32.100
If you're a computer programmer,

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:35.910
you need to know how you got
the data in computer memory

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:38.130
that the measurement gave you.

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:42.983
So those describe where and
when and how big, what shape,

00:15:42.983 --> 00:15:45.750
and anything else the
experiment is gonna need,

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:50.583
strictly speaking ought
to be in that description.

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:55.260
So, now formally, in quantum field theory,

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:56.940
an f measurement and a g measurement,

00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:58.770
which is how I term them,

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:01.530
are if those are space-like
or causally separated,

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:03.120
then they're compatible operators,

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:06.570
in which case this expression,
get the right button,

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:08.463
is a correlation coefficient.

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:12.840
It's just as you'd expect
in classical statistics,

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:14.340
classical probability theory.

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:18.630
But if they are time-like
or light-like separated,

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:20.880
then they are incompatible,

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:23.523
and this construction has
an imaginary component,

00:16:23.523 --> 00:16:27.720
which I suggest we can
think of as a bookkeeping

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:29.220
from an engineering point of view,

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:33.497
a bookkeeping for the
consequences of quantum noise

00:16:33.497 --> 00:16:37.560
for this particular pair of measurements.

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:42.560
Now, we do have to work
with causal correlations

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:44.850
at time-like separation.

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:46.590
We have to have models for that.

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:49.440
What we use for that is the
collapse of the wave function.

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:52.980
It's just a mathematics for
constructing a joint probability

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:55.080
for constructing correlation coefficients.

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:58.260
This is obviously a way of, well,

00:16:58.260 --> 00:16:59.880
I term it in the third of the papers,

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:03.863
I decided at the top,
are a construction of

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:07.230
a joint probability construction.

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:10.040
And this is a way of rethinking
the measurement problem,

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:14.126
I find, and some people
have also thought so.

00:17:14.126 --> 00:17:19.126
So what I think quantum field
theory does is it, oh, sorry.

00:17:20.964 --> 00:17:24.510
For this CM+ electromagnetism,

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:25.920
it's empirically equivalent to,

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:28.942
because it's isomorphic to
quantum electromagnetism,

00:17:28.942 --> 00:17:30.750
the f measurement and the g measurement

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:32.490
are always compatible.

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:34.860
So you haven't got the
quantum field theory yet,

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:36.264
but you use the Poisson bracket

00:17:36.264 --> 00:17:38.880
to introduce the transformation algebra

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.335
and use it in a general enough way

00:17:40.335 --> 00:17:43.230
that it gives you all of the operators

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:44.910
you'd expect in quantum field theory.

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:46.710
And you can do it, it's isomorphic.

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:47.860
It's published, anyway.

00:17:49.890 --> 00:17:52.350
And so finally, I think
we can think of this

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:55.447
as quantum field theory
folding into the formalism,

00:17:55.447 --> 00:17:58.830
just the quantum noise
aspect of causality.

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:00.750
This is something that's reasonable to do,

00:18:00.750 --> 00:18:03.180
but it's also something you
don't necessarily have to do.

00:18:03.180 --> 00:18:06.480
You could make a conventional
choice of using CM+EM,

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:09.090
if that's more helpful,
if it's good to keep

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:11.175
your quantum noise separate.

00:18:11.175 --> 00:18:13.660
Okay, so six months ago,

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:15.840
because of machine learning
and causal modeling,

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:17.880
and because of Franke, damn it,

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:19.830
I really started to wonder whether

00:18:19.830 --> 00:18:23.220
physics really needs to be
about more than probability.

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:27.390
Because machine learning, it seemed to me,

00:18:27.390 --> 00:18:29.040
doesn't necessarily try to classify data

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:30.150
into reference classes.

00:18:30.150 --> 00:18:32.648
It doesn't necessarily count anything.

00:18:32.648 --> 00:18:34.950
So machine learning algorithm

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:37.830
just takes a data item
in its initial state

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:39.900
and constructs a state after that,

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:41.730
and that need not be any kind of linearity

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:43.230
or guided by probability distribution,

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:44.530
probability theory at all.

00:18:45.530 --> 00:18:48.360
So relative frequency,
just not part of it,

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:49.233
this whole thing.

00:18:50.910 --> 00:18:52.650
And on the other hand, there are certainly

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:54.270
generative large language models

00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:57.690
typically are constrained to
be consistent with probability,

00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:00.120
and it makes them much
more understandable,

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:00.953
and that's part, I think,

00:19:00.953 --> 00:19:03.000
of why people have adopted
an interest in them,

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.900
because they feel that it can
be understood at some level.

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:09.750
So if an algorithm is
consistent with probability, 

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:11.940
I suppose we might call it
Bayesian machine learning

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:13.860
because it does try to keep,

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:15.780
does make it more understandable.

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:17.130
But I do wonder whether it's better,

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:20.850
it is might be better to
keep very clear in one's mind

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:24.207
as to whether you want to go
that way or the other way.

00:19:24.207 --> 00:19:26.130
I'm not saying you shouldn't
go one way or the other,

00:19:26.130 --> 00:19:28.920
just it is something to keep in mind.

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:30.086
Okay, so I'm gonna go back for a moment

