00:25:45.240 --> 00:25:48.630
- So any questions before
we open up the floor

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:50.373
for our closing?

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:52.650
David?

00:25:52.650 --> 00:25:53.483
- Peter, thank you.

00:25:53.483 --> 00:25:55.320
That was really interesting,
and I might've missed it,

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:58.890
so maybe I'm really collapsing
things much too far.

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:01.260
But for your last point
here about quantum noise

00:26:01.260 --> 00:26:02.940
and its consequence being
modeled classically,

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:07.940
is that because what matters
to the dynamics of the classic,

00:26:08.250 --> 00:26:12.060
of the object we already
chose to treat classically,

00:26:12.060 --> 00:26:13.770
is 'cause those dynamics are sensitive

00:26:13.770 --> 00:26:16.830
to things like two-point
functions of quantum fluctuations,

00:26:16.830 --> 00:26:18.630
which we can then presumably, at least,

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:20.340
under many conditions, replace with

00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:23.460
a corresponding classical object?

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:25.890
Or is that missing more of the structure

00:26:25.890 --> 00:26:26.723
that you had in mind?

00:26:26.723 --> 00:26:28.241
I'm wondering, this
last part in particular.

00:26:28.241 --> 00:26:30.962
We do that all the time as a convenience

00:26:30.962 --> 00:26:32.880
when trying to model things on,

00:26:32.880 --> 00:26:34.586
admittedly, classical computers.

00:26:34.586 --> 00:26:39.586
We'll initialize a simulation
by matching the statistics

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:41.640
of two-point correlation functions,

00:26:41.640 --> 00:26:43.860
even though we're gonna then evolved them

00:26:43.860 --> 00:26:45.300
perfectly classically,

00:26:45.300 --> 00:26:46.950
but we think they had a, in some sense,

00:26:46.950 --> 00:26:50.160
capturing a kind of quantum
mechanical boundary,

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:50.993
something like that.

00:26:50.993 --> 00:26:53.654
Is there more than that, or
is that a reasonable analogy?

00:26:53.654 --> 00:26:55.680
- There is.
- Okay.

00:26:55.680 --> 00:27:00.203
- The way I think about,
this is, for one thing,

00:27:00.203 --> 00:27:03.180
correlations at space-like separation,

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:04.320
you can consider classically

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:07.011
because the operators do commute.

00:27:07.011 --> 00:27:10.302
At time-like separation,
they don't commute,

00:27:10.302 --> 00:27:13.743
but we still have
correlations we need to model.

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:17.223
So the way we model that is use collapse.

00:27:18.300 --> 00:27:20.850
But as far as the classical
physics is concerned,

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:24.000
it's just you use commuting
operators to model that.

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:28.020
So in the signal analysis sort
of framework, it's extremely,

00:27:28.020 --> 00:27:31.320
it's really quite natural to do that.

00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:34.890
But the analyticity I
mentioned on the second slide

00:27:34.890 --> 00:27:38.610
is critical to whether you
want to use the mathematics

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:40.350
or quantum field theory or not,

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:43.770
because analyticity is
mathematically so beautiful.

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:46.860
You don't want to throw that away at all.

00:27:46.860 --> 00:27:49.110
But on the other hand, if
you're doing signal analysis,

00:27:49.110 --> 00:27:53.656
maybe the cost of having a
hundred million collapses

00:27:53.656 --> 00:27:56.850
in the mathematics, you
know, just in the model,

00:27:56.850 --> 00:28:00.570
might over overturn the
advantage of using analyticity.

00:28:00.570 --> 00:28:01.975
- A quick follow up?
- Yeah.

00:28:01.975 --> 00:28:03.833
- [David] For that, that was very helpful,

00:28:03.833 --> 00:28:05.558
even just the gesture right,

00:28:05.558 --> 00:28:07.821
and I wonder-
- It's not a Wick rotation.

00:28:07.821 --> 00:28:10.860
- No, I won't even make the joke.

00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:13.710
But for, have you already thought about

00:28:13.710 --> 00:28:15.480
things like the Leggett-Garg inequality,

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:17.790
which as you may know is
a Bell-like inequality,

00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:19.800
but for correlation of measurements

00:28:19.800 --> 00:28:21.000
on a single system over time,

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:23.629
it sounds like that should,
might be a way to break

00:28:23.629 --> 00:28:24.750
what otherwise looks like in isomorphism.

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:26.940
But you might have thought
about all that as well.

00:28:26.940 --> 00:28:28.913
- I'm just gonna say to
you, but it's isomorphic,

00:28:28.913 --> 00:28:32.220
so anything that quantum
quantum field theory can model,

00:28:32.220 --> 00:28:34.541
can be modeled by a different model

00:28:34.541 --> 00:28:36.600
or what looks like a different model.

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:40.590
But really, the isomorphism
is completely non-local

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:43.620
because it is a projection
to positive frequency.

00:28:43.620 --> 00:28:46.980
And so it just, in some sense,

00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:50.430
it puts you in still an
interpretive mess, as far as the,

00:28:50.430 --> 00:28:52.500
but nonetheless, it's just an alternative.

00:28:52.500 --> 00:28:54.921
It's a different way of looking at it.

00:28:54.921 --> 00:28:57.360
And it's significantly different, I think.

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:01.530
And like I said on the first
slide in the pale gray,

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:05.790
this, I think, does give a
way to rethink renormalization

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:09.033
as well as rethink the measurement theory.

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:11.680
- [Priya] Thanks.

00:29:12.695 --> 00:29:13.980
Chris, and then...

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:17.160
- Or just that you had this broader band,

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:20.520
oh you called it an artifact,
so I was just wondering,

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:22.201
you know that it's an artifact

00:29:22.201 --> 00:29:23.550
or you assume it's an artifact?

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:27.630
